-- title:
--   Definitions 5.1–5.2 — strong derivatives of Schwartz-valued curves; Schwartz solutions of the heat (5.2) and Schrödinger (5.13) problems
-- statement:
--   A curve $u : I \to \mathcal S(\mathbb{R}^n)$ (with $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ an interval) has **strong derivative** $v \in \mathcal S$ at $t$ if
--   $$\frac{u(t+h) - u(t)}{h} \to v \quad\text{in } \mathcal S \text{ as } h \to 0,\ t+h \in I,$$
--   convergence being in the Schwartz topology (all seminorms $\sup |x^\alpha \partial^\beta f|$). The curve is in $C^\infty(I;\mathcal S)$ if it has continuous strong derivatives of all orders (one-sided at endpoints).
--
--   A **Schwartz solution of the heat problem** $u_t = \Delta u$, $u(0) = f$ on an interval $I \ni 0$ ($I = [0,\infty)$ or $[0,T]$), for real $f \in \mathcal S$, is a curve $u \in C(I;\mathcal S) \cap C^1(\operatorname{int} I;\mathcal S)$ with $u(0) = f$ and $u_t(t) = \Delta u(t)$ in $\mathcal S$ for every interior $t$. A **Schwartz solution of the Schrödinger problem** $iu_t = -\Delta u$, $u(0)=f$, for complex $f \in \mathcal S$, is $u \in C^1(\mathbb{R};\mathcal S)$ with $u(0) = f$ and $i\,u_t(t) = -\Delta u(t)$ for every $t \in \mathbb{R}$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** $\mathcal S$ is Mathlib's `SchwartzMap` with its Fréchet topology, which coincides with the book's (5.72). The derivative is the limit of difference quotients along the punctured neighbourhood of $0$ restricted to $t + h \in I$; the solution predicates require a two-sided strong derivative at interior times, continuous there, and the Laplacian is Mathlib's $\Delta : \mathcal S \to \mathcal S$. $C^\infty(I;\mathcal S)$ is encoded by a sequence of curves $D_j = \partial_t^j u$.
-- source:
--   Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations (revised 6/18/2014), p. 128, Definitions 5.1–5.2; p. 129 (the interpretation of (5.2) before Theorem 5.4); p. 138, Eq. (5.13)

import Mathlib

namespace HunterPDE.HeatFourier

open Filter Topology
open scoped SchwartzMap Laplacian

variable {n : ℕ} {F : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup F] [NormedSpace ℝ F]

/-- Strong (Schwartz-space) derivative of a curve `u : ℝ → 𝓢(ℝⁿ, F)` relative to a set `I` of
times, Hunter, *Notes on PDEs*, Definition 5.1 (p. 128): `v ∈ 𝓢` is the derivative of `u` at `t`
if the difference quotients converge **in the Schwartz topology**,
`(u(t + h) − u(t)) / h → v` in `𝓢` as `h → 0` with `h ≠ 0` and `t + h ∈ I`.
With `I = Set.univ` (or `t` interior to `I`) this is the two-sided derivative of Definition 5.1;
at an endpoint of an interval it is the one-sided derivative of the "obvious modifications"
after Definition 5.2. The topology of `𝓢` is Mathlib's, generated by the seminorms
`sup |x|ᵏ ‖Dˡ f(x)‖`, the same topology as the book's seminorms (5.72). -/
def HasStrongDerivWithinAt (u : ℝ → 𝓢(EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n), F))
    (v : 𝓢(EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n), F)) (I : Set ℝ) (t : ℝ) : Prop :=
  Tendsto (fun h : ℝ => h⁻¹ • (u (t + h) - u t)) (𝓝[{h | h ≠ 0 ∧ t + h ∈ I}] 0) (𝓝 v)

/-- `u ∈ C^∞(I; 𝓢)` (Hunter, *Notes on PDEs*, Definition 5.2, p. 128): `u` has continuous strong
derivatives of all orders on the interval `I`. Encoded as a sequence of curves `D j = ∂ₜʲ u`
with `D 0 = u`, each `D j` continuous on `I` (in the topology of `𝓢`) and strongly
differentiable at every `t ∈ I` relative to `I` with derivative `D (j + 1) t`
(one-sided at an endpoint of `I`). -/
def IsStrongSmoothOn (u : ℝ → 𝓢(EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n), F)) (I : Set ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∃ D : ℕ → ℝ → 𝓢(EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n), F), D 0 = u ∧
    ∀ j : ℕ, ContinuousOn (D j) I ∧ ∀ t ∈ I, HasStrongDerivWithinAt (D j) (D (j + 1) t) I t

/-- A Schwartz-valued solution of the heat initial value problem (5.2) on a time interval `I`
(`I = [0, ∞)` in Theorem 5.4, `I = [0, T]` in Theorem 5.10), in the sense stated before
Theorem 5.4 (Hunter, *Notes on PDEs*, p. 129) and in (5.3):
`u ∈ C(I; 𝓢) ∩ C¹(int I; 𝓢)`, `u(0) = f`, and for every interior time `t` the strong derivative
`uₜ(t)` exists and equals `Δu(t)`, where `Δ : 𝓢 → 𝓢` is the Laplacian.
Here: `u` is continuous on `I` in the topology of `𝓢`; there is a curve `uₜ` which is the
(two-sided) strong derivative of `u` at each `t` in the interior of `I` and is continuous there;
and `uₜ(t) = Δ(u(t))` in `𝓢`. Data and solution are real-valued, as in §5.1.1. -/
def IsSchwartzHeatSolution (I : Set ℝ) (f : 𝓢(EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n), ℝ))
    (u : ℝ → 𝓢(EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n), ℝ)) : Prop :=
  ContinuousOn u I ∧ u 0 = f ∧
    ∃ ut : ℝ → 𝓢(EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n), ℝ),
      (∀ t ∈ interior I, HasStrongDerivWithinAt u (ut t) Set.univ t) ∧
      ContinuousOn ut (interior I) ∧
      ∀ t ∈ interior I, ut t = Δ (u t)

/-- A Schwartz-valued solution `u ∈ C¹(ℝ; 𝓢)` of the Schrödinger initial value problem (5.13)
(Hunter, *Notes on PDEs*, p. 138): `i uₜ = −Δu` for `t ∈ ℝ`, `u(0) = f`, with complex-valued
`u`. The strong derivative (Definition 5.1) exists at every `t ∈ ℝ`, is continuous in `t`, and
`i · uₜ(t) = −Δ(u(t))` in `𝓢(ℝⁿ, ℂ)`. The book's theorems ask in addition `u ∈ C^∞(ℝ; 𝓢)`,
which they state with `IsStrongSmoothOn u Set.univ`. -/
def IsSchwartzSchrodingerSolution (f : 𝓢(EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n), ℂ))
    (u : ℝ → 𝓢(EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n), ℂ)) : Prop :=
  Continuous u ∧ u 0 = f ∧
    ∃ ut : ℝ → 𝓢(EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n), ℂ),
      (∀ t : ℝ, HasStrongDerivWithinAt u (ut t) Set.univ t) ∧
      Continuous ut ∧
      ∀ t : ℝ, Complex.I • ut t = -Δ (u t)

end HunterPDE.HeatFourier


